<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">University of Marburg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1527">1527</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Philipps University of Marburg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marburg">Marburg</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, was founded by Landgrave Philip I of Hesse in 1527 and is the oldest Protestant-founded university still in existence. It trained clergy and officials for the Hessian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> and was named the Philipps-Universität after its founder. Later scholars there included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brothers-grimm">Brothers Grimm</a> as students, the chemist Robert Bunsen and the theologian Rudolf Bultmann.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/university-of-marburg.jpg" alt="University of Marburg" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Philipps-Universität Marburg · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Universit%C3%A4t_Marburg_Siegel.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
